#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5325439c-2662-58f8-9ac8-9c8ab16b4588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{d}_{1},{d}_{3}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374488} {:answer/id #uuid "3237331d-4d6b-5cde-b5d2-eb7e3885233f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374489} {:answer/id #uuid "e3af1383-f6b0-5db4-878d-e254e6226786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374490} {:answer/id #uuid "648b6f52-97fe-520e-9b02-9697cd97139f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374491} {:answer/id #uuid "f79e2ec3-c127-5401-b03b-c8956ad2ee35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "648b6f52-97fe-520e-9b02-9697cd97139f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374491} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d132632-debc-5831-8090-5c5876852c85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ef45091-e9f7-5655-9ce6-ad59a1b5ea21" :question/math-academy-id "q-111468" :question/problem "The matrix $D$ is given below. Let ${d}_{1}$, ${d}_{2}$, and ${d}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $D.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{d}_{1},{d}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(D).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${d}_{2}={x}_{1}{d}_{1}+{x}_{3}{d}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 24 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 24 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{16}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is ${{d}_{1},{d}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${d}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{2}}={{(-6)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=-6\\cdot 0=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9782760b-a53a-5e2e-a6a4-f1092e56495e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374495} {:answer/id #uuid "5095b33d-8d18-5baa-8d25-0cc49a0fa888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374496} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3ca8fc-fcf1-591e-9bc0-824c210f3cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374497} {:answer/id #uuid "fccfbfab-93c3-5533-99d9-f6853c7a56c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374498} {:answer/id #uuid "35e09f4e-de2f-55dd-91e7-c300712d0308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9782760b-a53a-5e2e-a6a4-f1092e56495e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374495} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b5dea3d-b36d-5d37-872b-d9901d3a2cb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5a3f3811-3d96-55cf-b222-418aca1a7192" :question/math-academy-id "q-111807" :question/problem "The matrix $R$ is given below. Let ${r}_{1}$, ${r}_{2}$,  ${r}_{3}$ and ${r}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $R.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${r}_{4}={x}_{1}{r}_{1}+{x}_{2}{r}_{2}+{x}_{3}{r}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 & -10\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $R$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$ is ${{r}_{1},{r}_{2}}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B={{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db3a4b60-1da6-552b-bfc5-1901fe77a653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374502} {:answer/id #uuid "c2fbd998-d8fa-5fe5-80dc-8e18f7f32669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374503} {:answer/id #uuid "4cbaada4-460b-53e7-b10f-d1407f49b7a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{d}_{1},{d}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374504} {:answer/id #uuid "db7a6b0b-fefd-528b-a7a9-fc835fc2cc15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374505} {:answer/id #uuid "233c4437-c3c7-5c42-8159-06e4c89f17e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2fbd998-d8fa-5fe5-80dc-8e18f7f32669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374503} :answer-field/id #uuid "09ba8c88-4ec9-533e-8915-61b77a478f4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "818d46d8-053e-504e-a7ef-dda21646e836" :question/math-academy-id "q-75944" :question/problem "The matrix $D$ is given below. Let ${d}_{1}$, ${d}_{2}$, and ${d}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $D.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{d}_{1},{d}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(D).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${d}_{3}={x}_{1}{d}_{1}+{x}_{2}{d}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -7 & -17\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $D$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -7 & -17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{8}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is ${{d}_{1},{d}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${d}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{3}}={{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{1}}+{{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=3\\cdot 2=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5788ede8-defe-5998-8e89-9fe460b2a93a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374509} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b2c5c5-ebb1-5fb2-a523-1b35ff675ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374510} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf3ba44-8cd1-5163-9f7e-7e2221ae0ab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374511} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a36221-4d90-5ef5-b164-d27b4fa3f4db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374512} {:answer/id #uuid "853be545-23e1-51e0-abca-118c27343825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "853be545-23e1-51e0-abca-118c27343825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374513} :answer-field/id #uuid "d087ea32-84ec-56d7-8d13-ebbc675e5e39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57b347bf-9941-5eb7-880e-06ec4f0acd68" :question/math-academy-id "q-75956" :question/problem "The matrix $W$ is given below. Let ${w}_{1}$, ${w}_{2}$, and ${w}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $W.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(W).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${w}_{2}={x}_{1}{w}_{1}+{x}_{3}{w}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nW=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $W$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is ${{w}_{1},{w}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${w}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{2}}={{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=(-3)\\cdot 0=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70e03ed2-c04f-5ac6-9481-08ad33c0d6c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374516} {:answer/id #uuid "25814223-9f1a-5872-9169-7741a7a797c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{v}_{1},{v}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374517} {:answer/id #uuid "af9422e0-df30-5156-84a4-dd2d595fdc25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374518} {:answer/id #uuid "8621ce1a-9fa9-5d25-94c4-ad650d7bcdac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374519} {:answer/id #uuid "0d0bfe15-577b-512f-8bb4-24cafdb791b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25814223-9f1a-5872-9169-7741a7a797c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{v}_{1},{v}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374517} :answer-field/id #uuid "8348dac4-cf4b-5637-952a-2b86f5a9be47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0a98068-9a46-5034-95cd-31840e044295" :question/math-academy-id "q-75980" :question/problem "The matrix $V$ is given below. Let ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$,  ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $V.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${v}_{2}={x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $V$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{10}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$"}]}
